## Ticket conventions and decomposition

Learn the project's beads conventions and detect overlapping work before proposing
tickets:

```bash
bd list --limit 30 --json
bd types
bd list --type epic --status open --json
bd list --all --json
```

Inspect plausible duplicates with `bd show <id> --long --json`. Reuse an existing
ticket in the coverage plan when it already satisfies the proposed scope; do not
create a duplicate or modify unrelated pre-existing tickets without user approval.

{{ template "beads-issues/shared/decomposition-work-item-quality" . }}

### Epic grouping

Group related work under an epic only when at least two work items share one clear,
specific objective. An epic is a coordination container: give it a concise goal,
completion definition, and child summary, but keep implementation acceptance
criteria on its children. Leave genuinely standalone work at the top level. Never
create a one-child epic or force unrelated items together merely because they touch
the same broad subsystem.

Build a minimal dependency DAG. Distinguish actual prerequisites from work that can
proceed in parallel. Ensure every requirement or approved plan step maps to at least
one proposed bead or an explicitly identified existing bead.